  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION & AREA FORECAST DISCUSSIONS...\n\nAs of 10 AM, the warm sector of the storm has pushed east and we \nare now advecting relatively colder air aloft. Looking at GFS 18z \nforecast soundings, there is some background instability only up to \n700 mbs. The axis of the trough to the north is passing east of the \ncoastal basin, making showers increasingly more isolated this \nafternoon through this evening throughout the coastal basin, with \nperhaps a few leftover showers in SD County tonight before \ncompletely drying out region-wide overnight into Monday. Given the \nrelatively shallow instability profiles, any showers that do develop \nwill be mainly light, with only a few brief showers of moderate \nintensity.\n\nMonday will begin with cold morning temperatures, especially in the \ninland valleys (40s), high desert (mid/upper 30s) and mountain \nvalleys (20s/30s). Otherwise, expect sunny skies across the region \nwith light N/NE offshore flow over the mountains and deserts during \nthe day, then increasing mid and high level clouds during the \nevening as another trough moves north of the region.  \n\nWith the weak trough moving through Tuesday, some light rain or \ndrizzle cannot be ruled out for coastal and inland SD County though \nthe one model (ECMWF ENS) picking up on this possibility seems to\nbe backing off.\n\nAs a result of this trough and another trough Thursday, high \ntemperatures will remain rather steady Tue-Thu: mid- 70s across \nthe coastal basin (including the IE), upper 60s in the high \ndesert, 50s in the mountains and around 80F in the low deserts.\n\nFriday through the weekend, an upper ridge builds over the Western \nUS. While the intensity of the ridge over SoCal remains uncertain, \nthe thermal trough likely sets up at the coast or a few miles \ninland, increasing high temperatures to the low to mid-80s in the \ncoastal basin and lower deserts, while the mountains and high \ndeserts remain cool.",
